I understand completely. Right now, when the Final Approver clicks the "Override Schedule" option, the code is forcefully resetting the time picker to Current Time + 5 minutes instead of letting them start from the Creator's originally scheduled time.
We can fix this by updating the onClick event for the "Override Schedule" box. We will tell it to use the Creator's time (if it exists and hasn't lapsed), so the Approver can simply adjust it.
Here is the exact fix for **VoucherAuthorization.js**.
### The Fix
Scroll down to the Universal Action Screen section, inside the routing options (around line 1180 in your code).
**Find this exact block for the Override box:**
```javascript
                            {/* Make Override take full width (md={12}) if the other option is hidden */}
                            <Grid item xs={12} md={(vidDetails?.SCHEDULED_TIME && new Date(vidDetails.SCHEDULED_TIME) < new Date()) ? 12 : 6} sx={{ p: 0 }}>
                              <Box
                               onClick={() => {
                                  setScheduleOverrideType("OVERRIDE");
                                  // Auto-fill to just 5 minutes from now as a polite default
                                  setFinalScheduleTime(dayjs().add(5, 'minute').format("YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm"));
                                }}
                                sx={jaStyles.overrideOption(scheduleOverrideType === "OVERRIDE", true)}
                              >

```
**Replace it completely with this:**
```javascript
                            {/* Make Override take full width (md={12}) if the other option is hidden */}
                            <Grid item xs={12} md={(vidDetails?.SCHEDULED_TIME && new Date(vidDetails.SCHEDULED_TIME) < new Date()) ? 12 : 6} sx={{ p: 0 }}>
                              <Box
                               onClick={() => {
                                  setScheduleOverrideType("OVERRIDE");
                                  
                                  // If the creator scheduled a time AND it is still in the future, prepopulate the time picker with it.
                                  // Otherwise (if it was immediate, or the time already lapsed), default to +5 minutes from now.
                                  if (vidDetails?.SCHEDULED_TIME && dayjs(vidDetails.SCHEDULED_TIME).isAfter(dayjs())) {
                                    setFinalScheduleTime(dayjs(vidDetails.SCHEDULED_TIME).format("YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm"));
                                  } else {
                                    setFinalScheduleTime(dayjs().add(5, 'minute').format("YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm"));
                                  }
                                }}
                                sx={jaStyles.overrideOption(scheduleOverrideType === "OVERRIDE", true)}
                              >

```
### Why this works:
 1. **Preserves Creator's Intent:** If the Creator scheduled it for 4:00 PM, clicking "Override" will now load 4:00 PM into the time picker, allowing the Approver to quickly adjust it to 4:30 PM without having to re-select the hours and minutes from scratch.
 2. **Prevents UI Errors:** If the Creator's time has *already passed*, putting a past time into the <TimePicker> will cause it to flash red and throw a validation error. The dayjs().isAfter(dayjs()) safeguard catches this and falls back to a safe +5 minutes if the time has lapsed.
